---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
---
<Base
  title="Catalogs, a first version — LightPhotos Blog"
  desc="LightPhotos now has catalogs: named lists of folders you switch between from the header. Your edits still live next to your photos. Here is what a catalog holds, what it leaves alone, and what it is for."
  active="blogs"
>
  <main class="lp-page">
    <article class="lp-article">
      <header class="lp-article-head">
        <p class="lp-article-meta"><a href="/blogs.html">Blogs</a> &middot; <time datetime="2026-10-13">October 13, 2026</time></p>
        <h1>Catalogs, a first version</h1>
        <p class="lp-article-dek">
          LightPhotos now has catalogs: named lists of folders you switch
          between from the header. Your edits still live next to your
          photos. Here is what a catalog holds, what it leaves alone, and
          what it is for.
        </p>
        <p class="lp-article-byline">Andrew Yao &middot; 4 min read</p>
      </header>

      <aside class="lp-tldr">
        <p class="lp-tldr-label">TL;DR</p>
        <p>
          A catalog is a list of the folders in the Folders panel. You can
          keep several, such as one for family and one for client work, and
          switch from the menu next to the LightPhotos name in the header.
          A catalog also keeps a copy of each photo's rating, flag and color
          label. Edits stay in the <code>.lightphotos</code> folder next to
          the photo, as before. LightPhotos will use what the catalog stores
          to search across all your folders at once. This first version only
          lists folders; search comes next.
        </p>
      </aside>

      <figure class="lp-figure">
        <svg viewBox="0 0 640 220" role="img" aria-label="Two photo folders, each with its own .lightphotos folder holding ratings and edits. Arrows copy only the ratings, flags and labels into a catalog, which also lists both folders.">
          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="0" y="12">YOUR FOLDERS (THE REAL RECORD)</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="24" width="300" height="84" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-t" x="14" y="46">~/Photos/Iceland/</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="30" y="68">.lightphotos/</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="88">ratings, flags, labels, edits, thumbnails</text>

          <rect class="lp-fig-panel" x="0" y="124" width="300" height="84" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-t" x="14" y="146">/Volumes/Work/Smith-wedding/</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="30" y="168">.lightphotos/</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="30" y="188">ratings, flags, labels, edits, thumbnails</text>

          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M300 66 H360 V110 H380"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-line" d="M300 166 H360 V122 H380"></path>
          <path class="lp-fig-arrow" d="M380 111 L380 121 L388 116 Z"></path>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="312" y="56">copy of marks</text>

          <text class="lp-fig-label" x="392" y="12">CATALOG</text>
          <rect class="lp-fig-box-accent" x="392" y="24" width="248" height="184" rx="8"></rect>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-t" x="406" y="48">work.lightphotos/</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="422" y="74">catalog.json</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="422" y="92">the list of folders</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-mono lp-fig-s" x="422" y="122">marks/</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="422" y="140">one file per folder: ratings,</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="422" y="156">flags and color labels</text>
          <text class="lp-fig-s" x="406" y="190">No edits. No thumbnails.</text>
        </svg>
        <figcaption>
          The folder is still where your work lives. The catalog lists the
          folders and keeps a copy of their marks, which it can rebuild at
          any time.
        </figcaption>
      </figure>

      <h2>Why catalogs</h2>
      <p>
        Until now, LightPhotos had one Folders list. Add every shoot you
        care about and it gets long. I keep family photos and other people's
        photos on different drives, and I want to see one set at a time.
        Lightroom users expect this too: Lightroom Classic has had separate
        catalogs for years.
      </p>
      <p>
        So the open catalog's name now sits in the header, after the
        LightPhotos wordmark. Click it for <strong>New Catalog&hellip;</strong>,
        <strong>Open Catalog&hellip;</strong> and your recent catalogs. Add
        Folder moved above the Folders list, so it stays in view however long
        the list grows. And once a catalog has folders, the home page stops
        asking you to open one; you pick a folder from the list.
      </p>

      <h2>What a catalog does not hold</h2>
      <p>
        In <a href="/blogs/where-your-data-lives.html">where your data
        lives</a>, I wrote about my first central catalog. It recorded every
        photo by its full path, and it lost your ratings as soon as you moved
        a folder. I did not want to bring that back.
      </p>
      <p>
        So a catalog does not hold your work. Every rating and edit is still
        written first to the photo's own sidecar, in the
        <code>.lightphotos</code> folder next to it. Move the folder, copy it
        to a friend, or open it in the browser, and the work goes with it.
        Delete a catalog and you lose a list of folders, not an hour of
        culling.
      </p>

      <h2>What it does hold, and why</h2>
      <p>
        Besides the list of folders, a catalog keeps a copy of each rated,
        flagged or labelled photo's marks, one small JSON file per folder.
        That copy follows the sidecars. When the marks in the open folder
        change, LightPhotos rewrites that folder's file in the catalog with
        the folder's current marks. If the copy is stale, visiting the
        folder fixes it. If it is lost, nothing is lost; the next visit
        writes it again.
      </p>
      <p>
        The copy is for the cost I named in that older post: a search across
        every folder is slow, because the app has to open each folder's
        sidecars. With the marks in one place, a later version can answer
        &ldquo;every five-star photo from this year&rdquo; without walking
        every drive. Search will read what the catalog stores, not the
        sidecars. Nothing reads the copy yet. I wanted it written, and
        correct, before search depends on it.
      </p>

      <h2>On disk and in the browser</h2>
      <p>
        On the desktop, a catalog is a folder named
        <code>&lt;name&gt;.lightphotos</code>, by default in
        <code>~/Pictures/LightPhotos/</code>. You can copy it, back it up, or
        open one from anywhere with <strong>Open Catalog&hellip;</strong>.
        If the catalog you used last cannot be read at launch, the app opens
        <code>default</code> with a message, and tries the last one again
        next time.
      </p>
      <p>
        The browser cannot keep a folder of its own on your disk, so the web
        app keeps catalogs in the site's storage, in the same JSON format.
        Its catalog menu lists every catalog made in that browser, and there
        is no Open Catalog. The browser also loads a catalog a moment after
        the page starts, so a folder you add or remove in that moment is
        merged in when it arrives, instead of being overwritten.
      </p>
      <p>
        If you used LightPhotos before this release, your Folders list is now
        the <code>default</code> catalog. Nothing to do.
      </p>

      <h2>What is missing</h2>
      <ul class="lp-article-list">
        <li>No search or filter across folders yet. That is the next step, and it will run on what the catalog stores.</li>
        <li>No way to rename or delete a catalog from inside the app.</li>
        <li>The Grid still shows one folder at a time.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The help page on <a href="/docs/catalogs.html">catalogs</a> has the
        steps. If you want something from catalogs that is not here,
        <a href="https://github.com/andrewyao/lightphotos/issues">open an
        issue</a>.
      </p>

      <nav class="lp-article-nav" aria-label="More posts">
        <a class="lp-article-nav-prev" href="/blogs/lightroom-alternative.html">
          <span class="lp-article-nav-dir">Back</span>
          <span class="lp-article-nav-title">A free, open-source alternative to Lightroom</span>
        </a>
      </nav>
    </article>
  </main>
</Base>
